This is the first draft of a description of xpost from the user side.
What can it do? How much of Postscript is written? How much is tested?

All of these are very good questions. Hopefully we'll address some of
them in the following.

Xpost is still in the "under-construction" phase. But I'm happy enough
with it that I think it should be considered an "alpha" status product.
The testing is not extensive, but it is in place, and has been used
throughout the development. 


== Download and get a PS> prompt ==

Requires a working gnu gcc installation with make and /bin/sh and
everything. Tested on cygwin, should work fine on Linux.

Clone the Mercurial repository with

  hg clone https://luser.droog@code.google.com/p/xpost/

Configure and compile all the source objects.

  ./autogen.sh (or ./configure if autogen.sh has already been launched)
  make
  make install   # or sudo make install

Run the interpreter. Type 'runtest' and enter to run the operator and 
graphics tests.

  ./xpost

loading init.ps...
loading err.ps...
Xpost Version 0.0.1
PS> runtest

Type `quit` or send an EOF (ctrl-D or maybe ctrl-Z) to quit
the interpreter.



== Examples ==

There are a few example programs demonstrating xpost's base
functionality without graphics. These are in the /ex subdirectory.

binshape.ps  -  draw an infinite output of random 0s and 1s
digit.ps - (doesn't work yet. uses <~ascii85~>, which xpost won't process (yet))
hwbin.ps - hello world using binary radix numbers
hwhex.ps - hello world using hexstring
tab340.ps - the smmaller golfed tablature generator
tab418.ps - the larger golfed tablature generator
tabref.ps - the large reference tablature generator
ubf119.ps - the 119 char universal binary-function lookup table
ubf70.ps - the 70 char universal binary-function lookup table

Some of these were written to make use of Ghostscript's argument
processing option `gs -- prog.ps arg0 arg1 arg2`. But xpost doesn't
do that yet, so you'll have to define

  /ARGUMENTS [ (A) (Bm) (F#m) (F#m) ] def

as an array of strings before you `(tab340.ps)run` or whatever.
Check the source to see if it uses ARGUMENTS to know if you need
to set this variable before running.

For example, to run ex/binshape.ps which is a silly little program
attempting to display vertical streams of 0 and 1 reminiscent of
'The Matrix'. Launch xpost from the /ex directory.

  PS> /ARGUMENTS [ 80 ] def
  PS> (binshape.ps) run

Other examples with graphics have been added to the /data directory which is
also installed with `make install`.

  cd data
  xpost dancingmen.ps  (some figures from a Sherlock Holmes episode)
  xpost sin1x.ps     (approximating a function graphic with bezier interpolation)
  xpost tea1.ps   (this one has some issues, try it with gs to see it)
  xpost bitfont.ps  (a non-caching Type 3 font using 8-bit images)

== Postscript arithmetic and function-calling ==

Just a brief review of what you should already know if you're trying
to use xpost. Postscript uses a Reverse-Polish Syntax where the
operator follows the operands.

  a + b      Infix Notation (ALGOL, C)
  ( + a b )  "Cambridge" Polish Notation (Lisp)
  a b +      Reverse Polish Notation (RPN)

Postscript does not use punctuation symbols for the operators,
but short words.

    add sub mul div
    idiv mod            2 operand
    atan exp

    cos sin sqrt
    ln log              1 operand
    abs neg
    floor ceiling
    truncate round

note:
%<-- Postscript comments begin with '%' and run till the end of line -->$

For example, if you define /a, /b, and /c to contain values for
the variables in the formula "ax^2 + bx + c",

    /a 25 def   % define the variables a b and c
    /b 10 def
    /c 3 def

and a definition for x,

    /x 12 def

you can calculate the value of the formula like this:

                   % stack picture:
    a x x mul mul  % ax^2
    b x mul        % ax^2 bx
    c   add   add  % ax^2+bx+c

== Stack manipulation ==

To turn this formula into a function, we need to take the input
for the function and define it as x before applying the formula.

    % x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
    /quadratic {
        /x exch def    % define x from stack
        a x x mul mul  % calculate quadratic as above
        b x mul
        c add add
    } def

When the function is entered, the input value is on the operand stack.

    number
   |------|

Then we push the (literal) name /x.

      /x
    number
   |------|

Unfortunately, `def` expects them to be in this order:

    number
      /x
   |------|

Hence `exch` is used to reorder the top two elements of the stack.
When reading a postscript sequence like `/name exch def` one way to
think of `exch` is like a pronoun whose antecedent is just below
the name.

    whatev  /x  exch  def
       ^         |
        ---------

The function so far isn't very general. So let's tease out a, b, and
c and make them parameters, too, instead of hard-coded names.

    % a b c x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
    /quadratic {
        /x exch def   % a b c   % define x
        3 1 roll      % c a b   % reorder elements
        x mul         % c a bx  % calculate the bx term
        3 1 roll      % bx c a  % reorder elements
        x x mul mul   % bx c ax^2  % calculate the ax^2 term
        add add                    % sum up
    } def

This brings us to `roll`. As you can see from the stack comments,
`3 1 roll` permutes three elements of the stack. The first
number is how many elements to manipulate. And the second number
indicates how many elements to move and in which direction.

    /a /b /c   % a b c
    3 1 roll   % c a b
    3 -1 roll  % a b c
    3 2 roll   % b c a
    3 3 roll   % b c a
    3 0 roll   % b c a
    3 2 roll   % c a b
    3 -1 roll  % a b c


    n j roll

    __ j > 0 __     move top j elements to the bottom of n

    n            TOS
     -------------|
    |       j     |
    |        -----|
    |       |     |
    V       V     |
    a b c d e f g h
   ^       |       |
   |       |-------|
   ^           |
    -<-<-<-<-<-


    __ j < 0 __   move j elements from the bottom of n to the top

    n            TOS
     -------------|
    |     j       |
    |-----        |
    |     |       |
    V     V       |
    a b c d e f g h
   |       |       ^
   |-------|       |
       |           ^
        ->->->->->-

    "Positive J to roll away", "Negative to bring 'em back".

So the next step in revising/optimizing our function is to remove the /x name
as well.

  % a b c x  quadratic  ax^2+bx+c
  /quadratic {
      4 2 roll     % c x a b     % reorder elements
      1 index mul  % c x a bx    % copy and multiply
      4 1 roll     % bx c x a    % reorder elements
      1 index mul  % bx c x ax   % copy and multiply
      mul          % bx c ax^2   % multiply
      add add                    % sum up
  } def

If we apply `bind` to the procedure, all the operator names will
be replaced by the operators themselves, and the function now
performs no dictionary lookups at all.

  /quadratic load bind ==
{ 4 2 -roll- 1 -index- -mul- 4 1 -roll- 1 -index- -mul- -mul- -add- -add- }

This function will now execute as quickly as possible,
and it will not interact with the current namespace
and it does not allocate any data.


== Arrays ==

It should be apparent from the last example that a procedure is
just an array, wrapped in curly braces rather than square brackets.

So we could construct the same procedure in the following way.

  /quadratic [
  4 2 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  4 1 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  /mul load /add load /add load
  ] cvx def

This is similar to the way bind constructs its bound procedure,
by loading the operator objects by name and embedding them in
the array. One must be careful in using the direct method shown
here. Some operators are implemented as procedures and will
not be executed if simply substituted as above. `bind` replaces
only names that refer to operator objects. A more general approach
would inject `exec` calls after sub-procedures.

For example, suppose we want (for some crazy reason) this procedure
to simply print its result, and not leave it on the stack.
There's an operator `=` to do just that. But ... `=` is a procedure!

So, this won't work:
  /quadratic [
  4 2 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  4 1 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  /mul load /add load /add load
  /= load %<-- NO! Bad! procedure will not execute
  ] cvx def

This will:
  /quadratic [
  4 2 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  4 1 /roll load 1 /index load /mul load
  /mul load /add load /add load
  /= load /exec load  %<-- must explicitly execute "procedure" operators
  ] cvx def

You see the convenience of bind. It performs all those loads for you,
but leaves the procedure invocation intact.

  /quadratic {
      4 2 roll 1 index mul
      4 1 roll 1 index mul
      mul add add = } bind def
  /quadratic load ==

{ 4 2 -roll- 1 -index- -mul- 4 1 -roll- 1 -index- -mul- -mul- -add- -add- = }


== Sub-prompts ==

Xpost's %statementedit special file features secondary prompts at each
newline, reminding you of which open constructs are awaiting a terminator.

$ ./xpost

loading init.ps...
loading err.ps...
$error
Xpost Version 0.0.1
PS>{
{.:}
PS<1>(
(.:)
PS<2>[
PS<3>]
PS<3>pstack
[ ]
(
)
{ }
PS<3>{{{
{{{.:}
{{.:}
{.:}
PS<4>


== Big pages, and holding one a band at a time ==

A page's raster costs its width times its height whatever is drawn on
it, and at large sizes that is what a run runs out of. Six devices can
take their page a band at a time instead -- pgm, ppm, pbm, tiff, png
and jpeg -- and hold one run of rows rather than the whole page. What
is held in place of it is the marks, so a big page costs what its
drawing costs rather than what its paper costs. Every other device
holds the whole page.

A page small enough to fit in one band is held whole, so this costs a
small page nothing and there is nothing to turn on. A device may be
given a mode after a colon to say otherwise:

  xpost -d pgm       -o page.pgm job.ps    banded where banding pays
  xpost -d pgm:whole -o page.pgm job.ps    the page held whole
  xpost -d pgm:band  -o page.pgm job.ps    the page held as its marks

The bare name is the only one of the three that is weighed. The other
two say which route they want at any page, so the three are compared
by changing one word.

What :band settles is that the page is held as its marks whatever its
size. How deep a band is still comes from the budget, so a page the
budget covers is held in one band that covers it, and CurBandHeight
reads 0 there just as it does for :whole. What bands an ordinary page
is a smaller budget, below.

A mode outside those two words is refused before the run begins,
naming what was given and what the device takes. It has to be: a
selection carrying any mode at all is one the weighing leaves alone,
so pgm:bnad accepted would turn the weighing off without saying so. A
device whose page cannot arrive a band at a time takes neither word,
so bgr:band is refused too. raster:FORMAT is a different selector on a
different device and still means the pixel format.

  -d record   selects the recording class itself. It names no device
              to play into, so it plays into the colour raster, which
              makes it the same as -d ppm:band. It takes no mode.

Nothing in a page description chooses any of this. PLRM Appendix G
calls banddevice an obsolete LanguageLevel 1 device setup operator
that should never be used in a page description, so a program asks for
a grayscale page with /OutputDevice /pgm and how its marks are held is
settled by whoever runs the interpreter.


== How deep a band is ==

  -b, --band-bytes=BYTES

How deep a band is comes from a byte budget divided by what one row of
the device's raster costs. The budget is four million bytes unless
this says otherwise, and that covers the sheets a page is normally cut
to, so those are painted directly: letter and A4 and A3 in colour all
read CurBandHeight 0.

The same number decides whether a page is held as its marks at all. A
page the budget covers arrives in one band, which is the page, so it
is painted on the device the run asked for and nothing is written
down. So lowering the budget is the way to band a page that would
otherwise be painted whole, and it is the only way: :band asks for the
recording route and not for a second band, and a page under the budget
takes that route and still arrives whole.

  xpost -d pgm --band-bytes=100000 -o page.pgm job.ps

bands a letter page at the default resolution into 163-row bands,
because a row of it is 612 bytes. The same run at 612 gets one-row
bands. A budget that will not buy a single row of the page in hand is
refused when the device is made, naming the budget and what a row
costs, rather than quietly taken as one row; and a budget that is not
a whole number of bytes from 1 to 2147483647 is refused before the run
begins, naming what was given and the range.

The budget bounds the marks as well as the raster: see below.


== Where a banded page's marks go ==

  -s, --spill=auto|never|always

  auto     in memory while they come to less than the raster banding
           the page saves and less than the band budget, and in a
           scratch file past either. This is the default
  never    in memory whatever they come to, touching no scratch file
           at all. What the page costs then follows its drawing with
           no limit
  always   in a scratch file from the first mark

The scratch file has no name, nothing else can open it, and it goes
when the run does.

Two bounds under auto rather than one, because they answer for
different pages. What banding saves is the tighter of the two for a
page whose bands save little, and it says nothing at all about a page
whose bands save nothing -- a page held whole has no raster its marks
could cost more than, which is what a page reached with :band under
the budget is. The band budget answers for that page and for every
other, so a page held as its marks costs a band of raster and a
budget's worth of marks whatever is drawn on it.

Under auto and always, whether a scratch file can be made is settled
when the run starts, by making one and writing to it -- a permissions
check would say yes for a directory on a full filesystem, for one a
system-call filter refuses, and for one mounted read-only. Under
always a run that cannot have one is refused there with an ioerror.
Under auto the run is told once, naming the directory and the reason
the system gave, and goes on; a page that then needs to spill holds
its marks in memory instead. That message goes out at the log's error
level, so -q does not gate it: -q sets the interpreter's own quiet
flag and does not touch the log level at all.

Under never nothing reaches the scratch directory, not even to look,
which is one of the reasons the state exists -- a sandbox forbidding
file creation wants it. What is being chosen is unbounded memory.
Nothing else ever selects never: a scratch directory that refuses a
file does not demote a run to it.


== What a program can ask about it ==

currentsystemparams reports four names. All four are reported and none
can be set, for the reason above.

  MaxBandBytes    what one band of a page may cost this run, in bytes
                  of raster held at once: the budget --band-bytes set,
                  or four million where it set none

  CurBandHeight   the rows the device now being painted into holds of
                  its page at once, and 0 where it holds the page
                  whole

  RecordSpill     what this run asked for: /never, /auto or /always

  CurRecordSpill  where the page in hand actually has its marks:
                  /memory, /file, or /refused, meaning in memory
                  having been refused a file it wanted

Two names for the spill rather than one, because they can differ: a
run that asked to be weighed, on a machine that will take no scratch
file, reports auto and refused, and a reader given only the first
could not tell that from a page that never needed to spill.

The two band names are a limit and what it bought, and reading them
together is how a run's banding is checked: MaxBandBytes divided by
what one row of the raster costs is CurBandHeight, and a budget that
buys every row of the page reads 0 for the second, meaning the page
was held whole. At the default budget a letter sheet at 300 dpi in
grey, which is 2550 by 3300, reads 1568; a 612 by 40000 strip in
colour reads 2178; and the ordinary sheets read 0.

